\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Limitations}
\date{2026-11-11}


\p{Current limitations are relative to the full goal in [[fdip-00AU]].}

\p{No physical environment map is constructed. \ref{fdip-003X} asks for
one. \ref{fdip-00G0} and \ref{fdip-00G1} give converging finite-range
families one normalized limiting law, at mass squared
#{\frac{\mu}{\kappa}}. \ref{fdip-00FY} with \ref{fdip-00FZ} select
exact representative shapes once their free inputs are fixed. These
comparisons leave the environment map, the physical values of those
inputs, a physical mechanism selecting symmetry and vacuum data,
and physical spacetime unconstructed.}

\p{The backgrounds form a groupoid that carries no site, stack or descent. A
local-to-global statement about background objects would need a coverage on the
groupoid, sheaves of morphisms over it and effective descent, which
\ref{fdip-004Q} states with its source; \ref{fdip-0069} proves a finite
local-to-global theorem for laws and notes that it gives no descent data for the
objects and arrows of the background groupoid.}

\p{Some finite Gaussian families extend to a continuum Gaussian
field. For #{0<p\lambda<1} the hierarchical cell density is
square-integrable (\ref{fdip-00FG}). For #{p\lambda\ge 1} no
square-integrable point density matches every depth
(\ref{fdip-00FL}). The gasket graphs with edge weight
#{\bigl(\frac{5}{3}\bigr)^n} have a compact resistance
completion (\ref{fdip-00EN}), and \ref{fdip-00F5} states
its counting exponent.
These Gaussian scalar fields do not by themselves supply interacting
or fermionic dynamics.
The comparison on a supplied circle of \ref{fdip-004J} controls one
fixed Fourier mode, which is not a continuum limit of the full induced law.
\ref{fdip-003X} states what a physical interpretation would require, and
[Discrete induction and its open interfaces](fdip-003W) records which
comparisons remain open.}

\p{For polynomial interactions bounded below, \ref{fdip-00GI}
gives actual positive finite-dimensional laws and exact marginals.
The formal coefficients acquire the finite-order, compact-set bounds of
\ref{fdip-00GN}; they do not give a convergent infinite series or a
globally stable truncated density, as \ref{fdip-00GQ} explains.
The compressed vacuum evolution is a semigroup exactly when the retained
subspace is invariant under the full vacuum semigroup, by \ref{fdip-00HI}.}

\p{For bosonic scalar fields on finite spatial graphs, \ref{fdip-00GW} gives positive
interacting transfer and \ref{fdip-00GY} gives temporal reflection
positivity. The self-adjoint scalar Hamiltonian of \ref{fdip-00H2}
generates unitary real-time evolution by \ref{fdip-00HE}, with
finite-volume vacuum and thermal correlations in \ref{fdip-00HB}.
These statements require the declared coercive potential and time
conventions. They do not themselves give Lorentz covariance.}

\p{For the even quartic model with #{m_0>0} and
#{0<\lambda /m_0^2<\eta}, where \ref{fdip-00IC} specifies #{\eta},
\ref{fdip-00IH} constructs an interacting neutral scalar Wightman field in
#{1+1} dimensions. Its unitary representation of the proper
orthochronous Poincaré group, forward-cone spectrum, locality and unique
vacuum are continuum conclusions. The mesh is removed before the
spacetime volume grows, as in \ref{fdip-00I6}. This is a vacuum
construction; a continuum thermal state is not constructed here.
The result supplies neither an interacting spectral mass gap nor an
S-matrix, as \ref{fdip-00IK} explains.}

\p{Within the [compact gauge-transfer class](fdip-007Z),
strict kernel positivity gives a unique vacuum (\ref{fdip-00N6}).
Its correlations and long periodic-time limit are given by
\ref{fdip-00N7}; the self-adjoint Hamiltonian generates unitary
real-time evolution by \ref{fdip-00NA}. Retained gauge fields can
carry temporal memory, as \ref{fdip-00NF} shows. The vacuum is
determined by the supplied transfer data; this does not select the
physical group, action, metric or time step. These gauge results
leave Lorentz-covariant Yang–Mills reconstruction, a Poincaré
representation and relativistic spectrum condition, a continuum
gauge field and an S-matrix open. The correlation estimate supplies
no spectral gap uniform in volume and spacing. The neutral-loop
comparison in \ref{fdip-00NG} identifies symmetry channels without
deriving confinement, glueball formation or a physical spectrum;
it contains no quark fields or full hadron dynamics.}

\p{The [cyclic lattice duality](fdip-00NH) is an exact finite sum
over every torus flux sector under its ring and root hypotheses.
Its [finite-center comparison](fdip-00NR) does not establish
non-Abelian gauge duality or supersymmetric S-duality.}

\p{For the finite massive graded Wilson class, the QED comparison uses a
positive #{U(1)} connection marginal (\ref{fdip-00IP}), exact Grassmann source
integration (\ref{fdip-00IV}), vector Ward identities (\ref{fdip-00J2}) and
dressed observations (\ref{fdip-00J5}). Under graded total inversion (\ref{fdip-019F})
the massive Wilson determinant agrees at the two related connections.
If the connection probability is invariant under time reflection and
total inversion, the latter carries the reflected pairing from one
closed time half to the other (\ref{fdip-019N}). Even an invariant
probability can have a negative reflected square (\ref{fdip-019O}).
Connection positivity supplies neither a fermionic Hilbert space nor
electron dynamics; fermionic reconstruction and continuum QED remain
open here (\ref{fdip-00JB}). Maxwell scaling
(\ref{fdip-00J9}) concerns a classical action on smooth backgrounds.
For the [graded index](fdip-013L), [Ginsparg–Wilson operators](fdip-0146)
and the [index in a gauge field](fdip-0151), no locality bound for an
overlap operator, no expression of the index in terms of fluxes beyond
the scalar cases of \ref{fdip-019X}, no gauge-field topological charge,
no continuum chiral gauge theory or cancellation condition for gauge anomalies
is proved.}

\p{The Wilson [bulk integer](fdip-019W) counts the oriented vertex
zeros with negative mass. On an even two-dimensional torus with
periods at least #{4}, the index in the scalar angle window of
\ref{fdip-019X} is the negative of that integer times the scalar line
flux and the signed Clifford multiplicity. In dimension #{2m\ge4} on even periods at least #{4}, for independent scalar fields in the ordered coordinate planes the index in the window of \ref{fdip-01CB} is #{(-1)^m} times the signed spin multiplicity, the bulk integer and the product of their oriented line fluxes. For a scalar field coupling different planes,
a replacement for that product on the full #{2m}-cycle remains open
(\ref{fdip-01A9}). At a matching odd mass each face with trivial transverse links carries
the corner modes of \ref{fdip-01AH}. Their signed count depends on
the even transverse periods (\ref{fdip-01AI}). They exhaust the
kernel when all transverse links are trivial and normal links are
constant across each layer. Under these link assumptions, away from its
matching mass each nonzero transverse corner subspace has the least-gain
bounds of \ref{fdip-01AK}. Reflection exchanges the positive and negative
parts of the operators for the original and reflected connections
(\ref{fdip-01AL}). Every eigenspace preserved at a self-opposite label by an antiunitary of square #{-\bbI} has an orthonormal basis of pairs (\ref{fdip-01AO}), the paired factors of a real Fourier Pfaffian can have either sign (\ref{fdip-01AT}), and the [inversion product](fdip-01AV) multiplies one common inversion sign per antiunitary pair in a simultaneous eigenbasis of the strictly negative spectral spaces at the self-opposite labels, with deformation invariance and classification left open. For every integer #{R\ge0}, there are two scalar fields whose links agree
throughout the wall neighborhood of radius #{R} and whose fluxes vanish in
both strips. Their wall half-signatures differ by #{2} (\ref{fdip-01B7}). When the interior restriction is invertible, the reduced operator has a correction
supported on the retained sites next to the cut, with values determined
by the inverse of the interior restriction and the crossing hops
(\ref{fdip-01B9}). The normal step uses spacing #{a_s}. It is strictly positive on
every transverse field background when #{a_sM<1}. At trivial links
and #{a_sM=1} its defining inverse is absent. For some masses above this threshold,
particular fields still give a positive step
(\ref{fdip-01BI}). For a positive step the energy-sign dimensions
equal the positive and negative dimensions of the graded slice
kernel (\ref{fdip-01BL}). For a fixed positive step #{T}, the finite-thickness
operator has the sign spaces of #{\bbI -T} (\ref{fdip-01BK}). Repeating the same strictly positive
self-adjoint step in the same frame gives the normal direction its one-slice
meaning as a [time direction](fdip-01BF). A many-fermion Hilbert
space, a positive transfer representing the full Grassmann expectations
and reflection positivity of the Grassmann weight remain open for this
class (\ref{fdip-01BM}, \ref{fdip-00JB}). Distributing each principal scalar plaquette angle equally among its fine faces preserves the flux and, on admissible even two-dimensional grids at the same dimensionless mass, the index (\ref{fdip-01BZ}). Subdividing each independent flux plane preserves the product-field index in the same dimensionless mass and angle window (\ref{fdip-01CD}). With the same oriented axes, spin-fiber, grading and dimensionless mass,
the free Wilson bulk integer for periods at least #{3} is preserved when
the number of even periods is unchanged, in particular on all-even
grids or for odd refinement factors. For an integer #{j\ge0} at #{aM=2j+1}, with nonzero
spin-fiber and trivial transverse links on the same bottom face, its
signed corner count is preserved exactly when #{\binom{e_T}{j}} agrees
on both grids, where #{e_T} counts the even transverse periods.
The vertex and signed boundary counts can change when a half-period label
first appears (\ref{fdip-01BZ}). Interpolation can move a mass past the
cutoff (\ref{fdip-01BY}). The centered operator does not commute with restriction to a coarser grid (\ref{fdip-01BQ}), and no blocking map of spin fields that intertwines the Wilson kernels is constructed. No non-Abelian index formula, continuum inflow theorem, anomaly
polynomial or chiral gauge theory follows.}

\p{The [finite-group cochain coupling](fdip-00SE) and
[paired coefficients over a ring](fdip-00TU) give a finite algebraic
matter interface. The reflection criterion of \ref{fdip-00XH}
requires a declared functional and half-subspace. In the
[two-cell sign model](fdip-00XN), the full paired functional has a
negative even gauge-invariant reflection square (\ref{fdip-00XY}),
while its connection marginal is reflection positive
(\ref{fdip-00XZ}). General fermionic reconstruction remains open
as specified in \ref{fdip-00YA}. For based cochains with trivial links
and a nonzero finite-dimensional constant complex Hermitian fiber,
take the counting adjoint of the coboundary. Then
#{\extd+\extdag} is unitarily equivalent to #{2\ui} times the full
one-component staggered difference on the doubled torus at unit
fine spacing (\ref{fdip-014X}). The comparison with coefficient
links and the physical-label comparison remain open in \ref{fdip-00W9}.
The rotation-only affine coupling leaves the frame relation and
a torsion-sensitive matter determinant unresolved
(\ref{fdip-00Z6}).}

\p{The [orbit field](fdip-00ZW) has prescribed scalar values
with fixed radius in the isometric real model (\ref{fdip-011H}).
Local gauge invariance gives the finite cancellations of \ref{fdip-010N}.
The single-link hopping factor in \ref{fdip-0114} and the onsite
fermion coefficients in \ref{fdip-011R} are algebraic terms.
For one edge, \ref{fdip-013H} determines the characteristic polynomial
from the declared kernel values and the double-coset sizes, and
\ref{fdip-013A} shows that environment constants rescale each sector
sum and leave that matrix unchanged. A larger complex has the orbit
count of \ref{fdip-0124} and no characteristic polynomial here.
A physical Higgs or Goldstone mass and an infinite-volume domain
of analyticity require further hypotheses and dynamics, as in
the comparisons of \ref{fdip-0107} and \ref{fdip-0118}. Even a
self-adjoint finite Yukawa kernel can give opposite determinant
signs on two backgrounds (\ref{fdip-011T}), obstructing a general
positive probability law. For two specified Hermitian coupling
products the word traces (\ref{fdip-018Y}) test whether an antiunitary
commutes with both.
The dimension of their multiplicity space is supplied. Testing a
prescribed charge after inversion (\ref{fdip-0196}) requires its actual
action on both products. Symmetry of the coupling maps themselves
and of the full weight requires their transformations as well.}

\p{No statement in these notes fixes a physical spacetime, a physical
parameter, a Standard Model equation or a gravitational equation.}
